.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_ALLOCATED_BYTES 3 "2026-09-16" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes \- report the memory a pool holds for its entries
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the single block that mp holds for its
entries and for the state of each entry.

This is what the pool costs, which ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3)
deliberately does not report: a thread-safe pool large enough to keep a
cache for each thread holds a reserve above the count that it was created
with, so its memory exceeds what its usable capacity accounts for. Use
this call to size a pool against a memory budget, so that you do not have
to repeat the reserve arithmetic yourself.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the entry block of the pool
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The call counts the entry block and nothing else. The handle itself is a
small fixed allocation, and the cache of each thread is a separate
allocation that the library makes when that thread first uses the pool;
the same reserve bounds those caches. An entry that the dynamic fallback
serves is not part of this block at all;
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) accounts for those.

For a pool built by ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(3), this
reports the part of the caller's buffer that the pool divided into
entries. Such a pool never receives a cache and carries no reserve, so
the figure is exactly the element count times the stride, plus one
status byte for each element, and the pool allocated none of it.

The value does not change over the lifetime of the pool.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the lock of the pool, which makes it a cold-path call:
do not use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
